# Building a feature

Work in this order. Each step has a check; do not move on until it passes.

## 1. Find out what exists

```sh
howdy routes         # every route, its controller and file:line
```

Read `src/{{.Name}}.gleam` (the `Services` record and `app`, which
registers every controller) and one existing feature under
`src/{{.Name}}/features/` that is close to what you are building. Copy its
shape rather than inventing a new one.

## 2. Generate the skeleton

Pick the generator that writes the most of what you need:

| You need | Run |
| --- | --- |
{{- if .Database}}
| data with a JSON API | `howdy create controller <plural> <singular> field:type ...` |
{{- if not .API}}
| data people manage in the browser | `howdy create pages <plural> <singular> field:type ...` (add `--api` for JSON too) |
{{- end}}
| data used only from other code | `howdy create repo <plural> <singular> field:type ...` |
| a table on its own | `howdy create migration <table> field:type ...` |
{{- end}}
{{- if not .API}}
| one page | `howdy create page <feature> <page>` |
| a page that updates live | `howdy create live_page <feature> <page>` |
| a reusable piece of view | `howdy create component [feature] <name>` |
{{- end}}
| routes that are not about stored data | write a controller by hand (see the howdy-controllers skill) |

{{- if .Database}}

Field types are `string`, `int`, `float` and `bool`; a bare name is a
string, and every record gets an integer `id`. Names must not be SQL
keywords (`order`, `user`, `key` ...) or Gleam keywords: use `orders order`,
`accounts account`. The plural and singular must differ.
{{- end}}

Always look before you leap:

```sh
howdy create controller notes note body:string --dry-run --json
```

then run it without `--dry-run`. With `--json`, `created` and `modified`
list every file touched, and anything in `manual_steps` is wiring you must
do yourself. Do it before going on.
{{- if .Database}}

To change fields later, do not re-run the generator or edit the old
migration: follow the howdy-database skill.
{{- end}}

## 3. Adapt it

The generator writes working CRUD. Now make it the feature you were asked
for:
{{- if .Database}}

- Validation lives in `<singular>.gleam`, in `validate`: add rules such as
  `validate.max_length(200)`, `validate.email()` or `validate.min(0)` to the
  field's list.
- New queries (find by email, filter, order, count) go in `<plural>.gleam`,
  next to `all` and `find`; see the howdy-database skill.
{{- end}}
- New routes go in `<feature>_controller.gleam`. Fixed paths (`/new`,
  `/search`) must come before ones with a parameter (`/:id`).
- A controller is only served once it is registered in `app` in
  `src/{{.Name}}.gleam`, ahead of {{if .API}}the `howdy.not_found` handler{{else}}the static catch-all{{end}}. The generators do
  this; a hand-written controller needs `|> howdy.controller(x.controller(...))`.
- Keep feature code in the feature's folder. The folders beside `features/`
  belong to Howdy's generators.

## 4. Test it

Generated features come with tests. Extend them for what you changed:
a test per rule you added, per route, per edge case (missing record, bad
input). See the howdy-testing skill.

## 5. Check

```sh
howdy check          # gleam format --check, gleam build, deps, gleam test
```

Fix everything it reports, including warnings, then run it again.
`howdy check --fix` formats the code for you. Finally look at `howdy routes`
to confirm the routes are what you meant.
